  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consolidation of existing evidence and commissioning of new research on technology.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Technology Evidence and Research will be used to consolidate learning and evidence across the portfolio, and commission new research to build a body of evidence at the international, regional and country level for responsible technology development. This includes:
i.	Locally driven and independent technology governance research to inform national and regional technology strategies and policies and international norms and standards. Understanding the unique technology governance opportunities and challenges from a developing country perspective, cutting across financing, skills and infrastructure and through the consideration of human rights  (inequality, exclusion, privacy), economic growth (labour, market forces, vendor lock-in), democratic institutions  (external influences, mis/dis-information, surveillance), the dynamics of power, threats to national security , conflict and crime. Bringing LMIC voices, values and experiences to the global technology debates.
ii.	Testing innovative approaches for technology governance
iii.	Macro level research on the development and application of innovative technologies and their impacts on society and the economy, to support more effective innovation financing, technology skills development, supportive infrastructure and market access. 
iv.	Research on international technology governance and regulation, including international cooperation models and approaches, case studies and lessons learnt on alternative models for governance and regulation (i.e., those developed in global south), evidence on how international rules and standards impact ecosystems in developing countries.
v.	Provide responsive, call down support for Posts to inform effective UK engagement on technology.</narrative>
  </description>
